../research/notification-pilot-contract.md:69:Keep default proxy delivery working while exploring native timed snoozing. Android's public listener has timed snooze but no public arbitrary unsnooze. Do not bypass authorization using hidden APIs or pretend proxy posting restores source identity. Demonstrate handling updates, explicit source cancellation while snoozed, expiry, process restart, schedule changes, and budget exhaustion before enabling any native-original path. If exact budget control cannot be maintained, expose the experiment only in a controlled test path and explain the limitation in the pilot report.
../research/notification-restoration-feasibility.md:7:The [notification listener API](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/service/notification/NotificationListenerService.html#snoozeNotification(java.lang.String,long)) supports timed snoozing and reports the notification again when that time expires. It has no public arbitrary unsnooze method. The expiry therefore cannot simply be treated as an authorization request to Farhan before Android restores the notification.
../research/notification-restoration-feasibility.md:13:In the inspected [AOSP SnoozeHelper snapshot](https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/a8fb9fe93efdebc4145e00934f42c91742f328de/services/core/java/com/android/server/notification/SnoozeHelper.java), cancellation marks the stored record cancelled. Reposting checks that flag and suppresses cancelled records. The method returning snoozed records copies all stored values, including flagged records. Do not infer continued validity from membership alone.
../research/notification-restoration-feasibility.md:15:The inspected [notification manager cancellation path](https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/fb8f76eca9079c34af3e14ee0a58bc10a580ec42/services/core/java/com/android/server/notification/NotificationManagerService.java) handles an absent active notification by asking the snooze helper to cancel it and saving policy. That branch does not dispatch another removal callback. This is source evidence to test on the target OS, not a guarantee across all OEMs.
../research/notification-restoration-feasibility.md:21:- A cancelled notification must never later be proxied or charged merely because its snoozed record is still visible to the listener.
../research/notification-restoration-feasibility.md:22:- Do not run a polling loop over snoozed notifications as a workaround.
../research/notification-restoration-feasibility.md:24:- Distinguish Farhan's own snooze/interception removal from explicit source or user cancellation.
../research/notification-restoration-feasibility.md:32:## Pilot build: what the snooze and icon experiments do
../research/notification-restoration-feasibility.md:36:- **Timed snooze demo.** Applies only to the exact packages `ly.com.tahaben.test.{messenger,storm,social,calendar}`. On arrival, Farhan calls the platform's [`snoozeNotification(key, 60000)`](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/service/notification/NotificationListenerService.html#snoozeNotification(java.lang.String,long)) and does not intercept, count or charge it. When Android returns it, Farhan leaves it alone. Settings shows the last 20 callbacks (time, kind, Android removal reason; no text or key), and named trace counters `farhan.experiment.snooze.*` count them. There is no unsnooze, and this path makes no claim about restoring queued items or honouring budgets. Nothing in the real-app path calls snooze.
